. So c8 bishop: from c8 to e1 is diagonal (c8 to e6 to ... but e1 is 7 ranks down). The diagonal from c8 to, say, a6, but e1 would be c8 to e1: c8, d7, e6, f5, g4, h3. Wait, c8 to e1 is a diagonal of 7 squares. Let\'s see if the bishop on c8 can attack e1.\n\nThe squares between c8 and e1: d7, e6, f5, g4, h3. Now, d7 is PWF1 (White pawn), so the bishop\'s path is blocked at d7 by a White pawn. So no, the bishop on c8 can\'t attack e1.\n\nAnother Black bishop: on c5? Wait row5: c5 is BBF1 (Black bishop, moved). So c5 is a Black bishop. From c5 to e1: that\'s a diagonal. c5, d4, e3, f2, g1. Wait, e1 is the king\'s square. So c5 to e1 is two squares right and four down. Wait, c5 to e1: c5, d4, e3, f2, g1. Wait, e1 is e1, so from c5 (c is 3rd file, e is 5th), so difference of 2 files. Ranks: 5 to 1 is 4 down. So the diagonal would be c5, d4, e3, f2, g1. But e1 is two squares further? Wait, no. Wait, e1 is e file, rank 1. So from c5 (c,5) to e1 (e,1): delta file +2, delta rank -4. So the slope is 2/-4 = 1/-2, which is not a diagonal (which requires 1/1). So actually, c5 and e1 are not on the same diagonal. Wait, no: for a diagonal, the absolute difference in files equals absolute difference in ranks. Here, files: e - c = 2, ranks: 5 -1 =4. 2 !=4, so not diagonal. So bishop on c5 can\'t attack e1.\n\nBlack knights: b8 NBF0, and f6? Wait row6: f6 is empty (row6: e6 PBF1, g6 empty, h6 PBF1). Wait Black knights: row8 has b8 NBF0 (Black knight on b8), and maybe others. Let\'s see.\n\nBlack knight on b8: possible moves. To attack e1, the knight would need to be two away in one direction and one in the other. From b8, e1 is 3 files right and 7 ranks down. Not a knight move. So no.\n\nAnother Black knight: where else? Row7 has f7 PBF0, g7 PBF0, so maybe not. Row5: f5 empty (row5: e5 PWF1?), wait row5: a5 PBF1, b5 PBF1, c5 BBF1, d5 empty, e5 PWF1, f5 empty, etc. So Black knights: perhaps on g8? No, row8 h8 is rook. Maybe Black knight on, say, f6? Not sure. Wait, the Black king is